{"name":"Sugar Sweet","hex":"#ecc4dc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sugar-sweet","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["sweet as sugar; very sweet"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sugar-sweet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sugar Sweet","blocks":[{"text":"\"Sugar Sweet\" is a song by American singer-songwriter Mariah Carey featuring Jamaican singer Shenseea and fellow American singer Kehlani. The song was written alongside Dixson, Donny Flores, Felisha King Harvey of Cherish, Mikhai Wesley Miller, and producer Harv. It was released on July 25, 2025, as the second single from Carey's sixteenth studio album, Here for It All, through her own imprint, Mariah, and a media company Gamma, impacting rhythmic contemporary radio the following month."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar_Sweet"},{"title":"Sugar","blocks":[{"text":"Sugar is a class of sweet-tasting, soluble carbohydrates, many of which are used in food. Simple sugars, also called monosaccharides, include glucose, fructose and galactose. Compound sugars, also called disaccharides or double sugars, are molecules made of two bonded monosaccharides; common examples are sucrose, lactose and maltose. White sugar is almost pure sucrose. During digestion, compound sugars are hydrolysed into simple sugars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg/330px-Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar"},{"title":"Sweetness","blocks":[{"text":"Sweetness is a basic taste most commonly perceived when eating foods rich in sugar. Sweet tastes are generally regarded as pleasurable. In addition to sugars like sucrose, many other chemical compounds are sweet, including aldehydes, ketones, and sugar alcohols. Some are sweet at very low concentrations, allowing their use as non-caloric sugar substitutes. Such non-sugar sweeteners include saccharin, aspartame, sucralose and stevia. Other compounds, such as miraculin, may alter perception of sweetness itself."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Strawberry_shortcake.jpg/330px-Strawberry_shortcake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweetness"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg/500px-Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg/1280px-Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg/500px-Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sugar cane plantation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png/500px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png/1280px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png/500px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sugars (e.g. glucose) and their polymers (such as starch, cellulose).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AXBio_illustration